            <ArticleTitle>Endovascular management of type B aortic dissection after attempted central venous catheterization.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>979.e13-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.avsg.2011.02.037</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 41-year-old woman, status postmastectomy for breast cancer had an attempted 8-F left subclavian vein chemotherapy port placed in her. She developed severe upper back pain radiating to the left shoulder. A computed tomographic scan and angiography revealed catheter placement in the left subclavian artery and a type B aortic dissection. A thoracic stent-graft was used to treat the aortic dissection. While removing the catheter, a covered stent was deployed to seal the arterial puncture and a balloon-expandable stent was placed over a persistent subclavian dissection. This case illustrates an example of the feasibility of endovascular management to treat serious iatrogenic access complications.</AbstractText>
